    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 185,
      "text": "MEXICO\n72. A more predictable energy policy that is more open to private sector participation\nwould boost competitiveness and investment. Reestablishing more market-oriented regulatory\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 185,
      "end_char": 383,
      "text": "frameworks would leverage Mexico’s large and diverse renewable energy resource base. It would\nincentivize investments to create a cheaper, more reliable, sustainable, and competitive energy\nsupply.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 383,
      "end_char": 551,
      "text": "73. Further steps toward carbon pricing would reduce greenhouse emissions. The\nexpansion in 2023 of the emission trading system (ETS) will be an important step towards\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 551,
      "end_char": 745,
      "text": "comprehensively pricing emissions. Ensuring adequate coverage, enforcement, and monitoring of\npolluting activity, introducing legally binding emissions caps, and introducing the planned auction\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 745,
      "end_char": 944,
      "text": "system for allowances are key to making the ETS fully functional. Other measures, such as increasing\nthe carbon tax, introducing feebates, expanding public investment in clean energy infrastructure,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 944,
      "end_char": 1137,
      "text": "and regulatory reforms could help Mexico achieve its NDCs.\n74. It is recommended that the next Article IV consultation take place on the standard 12-\nmonth cycle.\n32 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}
